1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:02,366
Your little sister tiptoes up behind you,

2
00:00:02,433 --> 00:00:05,733
cardboard mask on, arms raised, ready.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,233
You don’t even turn around.

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:08,433
“Hi, Mia.”

5
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:09,633
She groans.

6
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:13,800
A pigeon dives at your head,
you duck, without looking.

7
00:00:13,866 --> 00:00:16,266
A cyclist rings his bell from behind.

8
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:17,633
You step aside,

9
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:18,900
like a dancer.

10
00:00:18,966 --> 00:00:20,500
Mia stares.

11
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:23,133
“How… how did you do that?”

12
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,533
Then a seagull swoops for your sandwich.

13
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,066
You hold it high.
The seagull leaves, offended.

14
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:32,200
A normal human sees about 200 degrees:

15
00:00:32,266 --> 00:00:35,133
everything ahead,
and a little to each side.

16
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,433
Behind you?

17
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:37,300
Nothing.

18
00:00:37,366 --> 00:00:38,100
You?

19
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:39,633
Three hundred and sixty.

20
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:40,900
The whole circle.

21
00:00:40,966 --> 00:00:42,100
No blind side.

22
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:43,233
No back.

23
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,200
Here’s the rule.

24
00:00:44,266 --> 00:00:45,466
Your eyes got wider.

25
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:46,966
Your brain did not.

26
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:48,866
Hold up a thumb at arm’s length.

27
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:51,266
Only that patch is truly sharp.

28
00:00:51,333 --> 00:00:53,833
Your eyes jump about three times a second.

29
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:55,733
Your brain stitches the rest.

30
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:59,000
You can see everything,
but you can only look at one thing.

31
00:00:59,066 --> 00:01:02,200
So what if you had 360-degree vision?

32
00:01:02,266 --> 00:01:04,833
Plus, nobody sneaks up on you.

33
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:05,700
Ever.

34
00:01:05,766 --> 00:01:07,766
Your surprise party is ruined.

35
00:01:07,833 --> 00:01:11,200
Forty people hide behind the couch,
all spotted.

36
00:01:11,266 --> 00:01:13,566
“Mate, you could at least pretend.”

37
00:01:13,633 --> 00:01:15,200
Pickpockets are doomed.

38
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:17,300
A hand slips toward your bag.

39
00:01:17,366 --> 00:01:19,633
You see it before it arrives.

40
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,433
The thief apologises, then runs.

41
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:23,966
Driving lessons.

42
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:27,233
Normal drivers turn their heads
to check blind spots.

43
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:28,200
You?

44
00:01:28,266 --> 00:01:29,300
Never.

45
00:01:29,366 --> 00:01:31,733
The instructor stares in silence.

46
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,700
“Dude… you didn’t even look once.”

47
00:01:34,766 --> 00:01:35,733
“No need.”

48
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:36,866
Dodgeball.

49
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:38,866
Twelve balls from every angle.

50
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:41,333
You dodge them all, facing the wall.

51
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,266
Game over in four minutes.

52
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:46,866
Teammates cheer.
The other team is writing a complaint.

53
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:48,000
Basketball.

54
00:01:48,066 --> 00:01:49,666
Ten players on the court.

55
00:01:49,733 --> 00:01:52,966
You see the other nine at once.
A behind-the-back pass.

56
00:01:53,033 --> 00:01:53,866
Perfect.

57
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:55,700
The coach drops his clipboard.

58
00:01:55,766 --> 00:01:57,000
Football next.

59
00:01:57,066 --> 00:01:58,900
Twenty-two players on the pitch.

60
00:01:58,966 --> 00:02:00,900
A referee can’t see behind him.

61
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:01,866
You can.

62
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:05,500
Every foul, every shove, every dive.

63
00:02:05,566 --> 00:02:07,600
Nature already tried this once.

64
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:10,066
Horses see almost all the way around.

65
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:11,266
So do rabbits.

66
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:12,866
They’re both prey animals.

67
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:14,800
Danger can come from anywhere.

68
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:16,400
Welcome to the club.

69
00:02:16,466 --> 00:02:17,533
The catch?

70
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:19,133
Nothing is background.

71
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,800
A moth flutters left.

72
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:22,166
Attention jumps.

73
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:23,766
A car honks right.

74
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:24,633
Jump.

75
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:26,566
A flag flaps behind.

76
00:02:26,633 --> 00:02:27,666
Jump.

77
00:02:27,733 --> 00:02:31,100
Seeing everything means getting
distracted by everything.

78
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:33,366
In class, 30 faces:

79
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:35,833
every glance, every smirk,

80
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:37,833
every whisper behind a hand.

81
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,233
Then a man in a grey suit finds you.

82
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:41,500
Mr. Black.

83
00:02:41,566 --> 00:02:42,800
He smiles.

84
00:02:42,866 --> 00:02:45,200
“We need a human security camera.

85
00:02:45,266 --> 00:02:49,566
Forty thousand fans,
one concert, 10,000 euros.

86
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:50,933
Just watch.”

87
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
“Just watch?”

88
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:54,033
“Just watch.

89
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,800
And never leave your post.”

90
00:02:55,866 --> 00:02:57,166
But there’s a cost.

91
00:02:57,233 --> 00:02:59,633
A busier picture means a busier mind.

92
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,100
By noon, your head is buzzing,

93
00:03:02,166 --> 00:03:06,733
amazing and overwhelming,
and sneaky surprises are ruined:

94
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,500
the gift behind a back,

95
00:03:08,566 --> 00:03:12,200
the party behind a couch,
the scare from behind.

96
00:03:12,266 --> 00:03:15,000
Ever wonder why surprises feel so good?

97
00:03:15,066 --> 00:03:19,266
Dopamine cells fire hardest
when life beats the prediction.

98
00:03:19,333 --> 00:03:22,500
You can’t predict everything,
but you see these coming,

99
00:03:22,566 --> 00:03:24,266
so the spark never lights.

100
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:26,000
You smile a second late,

101
00:03:26,066 --> 00:03:27,466
or not at all.

102
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:29,766
Worse, there’s no off switch.

103
00:03:29,833 --> 00:03:32,666
And one night, it costs far more.

104
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:34,166
Mia notices.

105
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:35,500
Of course she does.

106
00:03:35,566 --> 00:03:36,466
She…

107
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:39,300
keeps a little notebook.

108
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:40,633
Scares:

109
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:41,833
zero.

110
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:43,100
Tries:

111
00:03:43,166 --> 00:03:45,033
412.

112
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,300
She holds it up, hopeful.

113
00:03:47,366 --> 00:03:48,733
“Just once, Leo.

114
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,366
Let me win once.”

115
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:51,766
You never mean to ruin it.

116
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:54,166
You just… always see her.

117
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:55,200
You laugh.

118
00:03:55,266 --> 00:03:56,633
The tired laugh,

119
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:57,733
not the real one.

120
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:59,366
She hears the difference.

121
00:03:59,433 --> 00:04:00,633
“Are you okay?”

122
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,433
“Just tired,” you say.

123
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,000
She grins anyway.

124
00:04:04,066 --> 00:04:05,366
“Tomorrow’s concert.

125
00:04:05,433 --> 00:04:06,433
I got a ticket.

126
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:07,733
Mask on.

127
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:09,566
Watch your back, Leo.

128
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:11,500
Oh, wait.”

129
00:04:11,566 --> 00:04:13,266
Everyone else, you just see.

130
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:14,400
Mia?

131
00:04:14,466 --> 00:04:15,500
You look.

132
00:04:15,566 --> 00:04:16,600
The concert.

133
00:04:16,666 --> 00:04:18,366
Forty thousand people.

134
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:21,066
Mr. Black’s voice
crackles in your earpiece.

135
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,566
“Watch Gate 4.

136
00:04:22,633 --> 00:04:24,066
Only Gate 4.

137
00:04:24,133 --> 00:04:26,633
Look away, and the deal is over.”

138
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:28,566
So you stare at Gate 4.

139
00:04:28,633 --> 00:04:30,333
Behind you, metal groans.

140
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,166
High above the crowd,
a stage light shakes.

141
00:04:33,233 --> 00:04:34,833
A bolt snaps.

142
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:36,500
Your eyes see it instantly.

143
00:04:36,566 --> 00:04:37,533
Your brain?

144
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,200
A quarter second late.

145
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:40,866
Noticing takes time.

146
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:42,166
Then you see her:

147
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:45,133
a cardboard mask, right underneath.

148
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:46,066
Mia.

149
00:04:46,133 --> 00:04:48,300
The light hangs eight metres up.

150
00:04:48,366 --> 00:04:50,966
It falls
in about one and a quarter seconds.

151
00:04:51,033 --> 00:04:52,500
Take away your quarter.

152
00:04:52,566 --> 00:04:53,933
One second left.

153
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,100
Mia is 15 metres away.

154
00:04:56,166 --> 00:04:58,333
You run five metres per second.

155
00:04:58,400 --> 00:04:59,466
Three seconds.

156
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:00,733
Can you reach her?

157
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,166
Not even close.

158
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:05,366
You can see everything,
but you can only look at one thing.

159
00:05:05,433 --> 00:05:07,500
Gate 4 or Mia?

160
00:05:07,566 --> 00:05:08,633
You look at Mia.

161
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,166
You scream her name.

162
00:05:10,233 --> 00:05:11,966
“Mia! Up!”

163
00:05:12,033 --> 00:05:13,900
Forty thousand heads turn.

164
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:15,900
She looks up, freezes.

165
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:17,800
A stranger yanks her back.

166
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:20,133
The light crashes where she stood.

167
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:21,300
Silence.

168
00:05:21,366 --> 00:05:23,966
Mr. Black snarls through the earpiece.

169
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:25,266
“You’re fired.

170
00:05:25,333 --> 00:05:26,300
The money?

171
00:05:26,366 --> 00:05:27,366
Gone.”

172
00:05:27,433 --> 00:05:29,100
Silence would be easy.

173
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:30,966
Instead, you point.

174
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:32,866
Bolt after bolt.

175
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:35,300
Six more, loose across the rig.

176
00:05:35,366 --> 00:05:37,133
Engineers climb up.

177
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:39,166
Every one is loose.

178
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:40,566
The show stops.

179
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:42,266
Nobody else gets hurt.

180
00:05:42,333 --> 00:05:46,666
The whole crowd claps for the kid
with eyes in the back of his head.

181
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:48,666
Mia clings to your jacket.

182
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:49,933
“How did you know?”

183
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,266
“I always look at you,” you say.

184
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:56,900
Weeks later, Mia tiptoes up behind you,
a brand-new mask.

185
00:05:56,966 --> 00:05:58,400
You close your eyes.

186
00:05:58,466 --> 00:05:59,466
“Boo!”

187
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:00,600
You jump.

188
00:06:00,666 --> 00:06:03,233
Really jump, a real scream.

189
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:05,866
Then you laugh, right on time.

190
00:06:05,933 --> 00:06:08,100
The spark finally lights up.

191
00:06:08,166 --> 00:06:09,466
In her notebook:

192
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:10,500
Scares:

193
00:06:10,566 --> 00:06:11,533
one.

194
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:12,566
Tries:

195
00:06:12,633 --> 00:06:14,400
413.

196
00:06:14,466 --> 00:06:16,000
You open your eyes.

197
00:06:16,066 --> 00:06:18,966
You can see everything,
but you only look at her.

198
00:06:19,033 --> 00:06:20,566
Is any of this real?

199
00:06:20,633 --> 00:06:21,900
Not the power.

200
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:24,066
No human sees the full circle,

201
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:26,000
but the limit is real.

202
00:06:26,066 --> 00:06:29,900
In 1999,
Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris

203
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:32,866
asked volunteers to count
basketball passes.

204
00:06:32,933 --> 00:06:36,866
About half never noticed a person
in a gorilla suit walking through.

205
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:38,866
Seeing isn’t noticing.

206
00:06:38,933 --> 00:06:41,433
The dopamine spark is real, too.

207
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,566
The overload
and the spoiled surprises are fiction.

208
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:47,666
Your brain uses about a fifth
of your body’s energy.

209
00:06:47,733 --> 00:06:50,066
Thinking harder barely changes that.

210
00:06:50,133 --> 00:06:52,233
Extra vision wouldn’t make you starving,

211
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:54,233
or let you predict everything.

212
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:57,500
So, could a mind ever watch
everything at once?

213
00:06:57,566 --> 00:06:59,900
That night, your phone buzzes.

214
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:01,666
Unknown number.

215
00:07:01,733 --> 00:07:03,900
“We saw what you did at the concert.”

216
00:07:03,966 --> 00:07:07,366
Then a second message:
“You’re not the only one.”

217
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:10,800
So, what would you do
with 360-degree vision?

218
00:07:10,866 --> 00:07:14,400
Tell us the sneakiest prank
you’d spot coming in the comments.

219
00:07:14,466 --> 00:07:16,333
And if you’d look at Mia too,

220
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:19,666
hit like and subscribe for more What Ifs.

